278
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:15,200
They did not hide why.

279
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,420
The man in charge of the convention told

280
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:20,260
the delegates the goal was, quote, to establish

281
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:21,960
white supremacy in this state.

282
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,760
Poll taxes and literacy tests followed, and the

283
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:26,700
voting rolls emptied.

284
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:30,220
Most black Alabamians and many poor white Alabamians

285
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:31,100
lost the vote.

286
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:34,580
And that constitution was amended so many times,

287
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:37,440
nearly a thousand times, that it became the

288
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:39,180
longest constitution in the world.

289
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:41,700
Seriously, it's longer than most novels.

290
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:45,420
But underground, something else was waiting.

291
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:48,080
Near the middle of the state, there's a

292
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:49,320
ridge called Red Mountain.

293
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:51,260
It turned out to hold iron ore.

294
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:52,860
Right next to it, coal.

295
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:54,740
And next to that, limestone.

296
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:57,940
Iron ore, coal, and limestone are the three

297
00:11:57,940 --> 00:11:59,260
things you need to make iron.

298
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:02,060
Finding all three in one place is incredibly

299
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:02,380
rare.

300
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,080
So in 1871, investors built a brand new

301
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:07,860
city right on top of them, and named

302
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:10,760
it after the great English industrial city, Birmingham.

303
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:13,600
It grew so fast that people called it

304
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:14,560
the magic city.

305
00:12:15,300 --> 00:12:18,280
To show off, Birmingham cast a giant iron

306
00:12:18,280 --> 00:12:20,720
statue of Vulcan, the Roman god of the

307
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:23,160
forge, for the 1904 World's Fair.

308
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:25,000
56 feet tall.

309
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:27,360
Still the largest cast iron statue in the

310
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:27,600
world.

311
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:30,660
When the fair ended, Birmingham couldn't pay the

312
00:12:30,660 --> 00:12:32,640
shipping bill, so the god of the forge

313
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:35,000
was dumped in pieces beside the railroad tracks.

314
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:37,480
Today he stands on top of Red Mountain,

315
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:39,800
with his bare backside pointed straight at the

316
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:40,500
neighboring suburb.

317
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:43,200
But the magic city had a dark engine

318
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:43,460
room.

319
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:46,400
Thousands of black men were arrested for tiny

320
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:49,320
offenses like vagrancy, and then leased out to

321
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:51,180
coal mines and work camps as prisoners.

322
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:53,400
They worked in terrible conditions.

323
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:54,580
Many died.

324
00:12:55,280 --> 00:12:57,100
Alabama was the last state to end this

325
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:59,020
system in 1928.

326
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:02,860
Meanwhile, in the town of Tuskegee, a former

327
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:04,100
slave named Booker T.

328
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:06,400
Washington had opened a school for black students.

329
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:10,400
In 1896, he hired a quiet scientist named

330
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:11,960
George Washington Carver.

331
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:15,000
Carver looked at the tired cotton fields and

332
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,520
said, plant peanuts.

333
00:13:16,860 --> 00:13:18,340
They put goodness back into the soil.

334
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:21,700
Then he found hundreds of uses for them.

335
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:24,920
Dyes, milk, plastics, cosmetics.

336
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:27,960
And no, he did not invent peanut butter.

337
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:28,580
Sorry.

338
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:31,560
Farmers did not want to listen.

339
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:32,900
Cotton was money.

340
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:36,760
Then in the 1910s, a tiny beetle crawled

341
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:37,520
in from Mexico.

342
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:39,060
The boll weevil.

343
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:40,800
It ate the cotton.

344
00:13:41,420 --> 00:13:43,760
Field after field, whole counties were ruined.

345
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:46,660
So in the town of Enterprise, the farmers

346
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:48,040
finally switched to peanuts.

347
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:50,140
And they started making more money than they

348
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:51,080
ever had from cotton.

349
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:54,480
So in 1919, the grateful people of Enterprise

350
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:56,300
did something no town on earth had ever

351
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:56,540
done.

352
00:13:57,300 --> 00:13:58,200
They built a monument.

353
00:13:58,900 --> 00:13:59,520
To the bug.

354
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:02,560
It's still there, in the main street.

355
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:05,280
A woman holding a giant boll weevil over

356
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:05,700
her head.

357
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,020
The only monument in the world to an

358
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:08,960
agricultural pest.

359
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:10,840
And just one more.

360
00:14:11,260 --> 00:14:13,480
In 1910, the Wright brothers came to an

361
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:16,600
old cotton plantation outside Montgomery and opened America's

362
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:18,140
first civilian flying school.

363
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:21,020
Their students made the first night flights in

364
00:14:21,020 --> 00:14:21,400
history.

365
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:25,680
Alabama farmers were watching airplanes before most Americans

366
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:26,940
had ever seen one.

367
00:14:27,460 --> 00:14:28,160
And the people.

368
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:32,300
Alabama kept producing people the whole world would

369
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:32,560
know.

370
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:36,560
In 1880, in the town of Tuscumbia, a

371
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:38,440
baby girl was born who would lose her

372
00:14:38,440 --> 00:14:40,380
sight and her hearing before she was two.

373
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:43,880
Years later, at a water pump in her

374
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:47,020
family's garden, her teacher spelled a word into

375
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:50,100
her hand, over and over, while cold water

376
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:51,280
ran across the other one.

377
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:55,560
W-A-T-E-R.

378
00:14:56,920 --> 00:14:59,900
That was the moment Helen Keller understood that

379
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:01,060
things have names.

380
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:03,480
She went on to write books and travel

381
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:03,900
the world.

382
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:06,320
And the list keeps going.

383
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:09,520
Jesse Owens, who won four gold medals in

384
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:11,120
Berlin in front of Hitler.

385
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:12,760
Joe Lewis, the boxer.

386
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:15,480
Hank Aaron and Willie Mays, two of the

387
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:17,240
greatest baseball players ever.

388
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:20,560
Nat King Cole, Hank Williams, all of them

389
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:21,640
born in Alabama.

390
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:26,010
And many of them grew up somewhere else.

391
00:15:26,650 --> 00:15:29,550
Because in the early 1900s, hundreds of thousands

392
00:15:29,550 --> 00:15:32,230
of black Alabamians packed up and left for

393
00:15:32,230 --> 00:15:33,390
the factories of the North.

394
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:37,330
For Chicago, Detroit, and Cleveland, looking for work

395
00:15:37,330 --> 00:15:39,090
and for a life without Jim Crow.

396
00:15:41,210 --> 00:15:41,770
1931.

397
00:15:42,530 --> 00:15:44,290
A freight train in northern Alabama.

398
00:15:45,310 --> 00:15:48,590
Nine black teenagers, the youngest about 13, are

399
00:15:48,590 --> 00:15:50,510
pulled off it and accused of a crime

400
00:15:50,510 --> 00:15:51,350
they did not commit.

401
00:15:52,350 --> 00:15:55,310
In the town of Scottsboro, all white juries

402
00:15:55,310 --> 00:15:57,110
sentenced eight of them to death.

403
00:15:58,030 --> 00:16:00,270
One of their accusers later took her story

404
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:00,650
back.

405
00:16:01,450 --> 00:16:03,330
The case went to the United States Supreme

406
00:16:03,330 --> 00:16:06,170
Court twice, and it changed the law on

407
00:16:06,170 --> 00:16:08,130
fair trials for everyone in America.

408
00:16:08,810 --> 00:16:11,230
But the young men lost years of their

409
00:16:11,230 --> 00:16:12,110
lives in prison.

410
00:16:13,170 --> 00:16:15,630
Alabama officially pardoned the last of them in

411
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:16,310
2013.

412
00:16:17,850 --> 00:16:19,270
Remember the name Scottsboro.

413
00:16:19,910 --> 00:16:22,490
It comes back in a very different way.

414
00:16:22,970 --> 00:16:25,450
Now keep the Wright brothers in mind, because

415
00:16:25,450 --> 00:16:28,130
the sky is about to become Alabama's thing.

416
00:16:29,030 --> 00:16:33,470
In 1941, at Tuskegee, the Army began training

417
00:16:33,470 --> 00:16:35,750
America's first black military pilots.

418
00:16:36,710 --> 00:16:38,750
Plenty of people in charge expected them to

419
00:16:38,750 --> 00:16:39,010
fail.

420
00:16:40,330 --> 00:16:43,050
Nearly a thousand pilots earned their wings there.

421
00:16:43,830 --> 00:16:45,730
In the war over Europe, they painted the

422
00:16:45,730 --> 00:16:48,230
tails of their planes red, and bomber crews

423
00:16:48,230 --> 00:16:50,770
started asking for the red tails by name.

424
00:16:51,850 --> 00:16:53,870
But Tuskegee holds a darker story too.

425
00:16:55,210 --> 00:16:59,070
Starting in 1932, government doctors studied hundreds of

426
00:16:59,070 --> 00:17:00,930
black men in the area who had syphilis.

427
00:17:01,690 --> 00:17:03,410
The men were never told what they had.

428
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:07,270
When penicillin became the cure, the men were

429
00:17:07,270 --> 00:17:08,089
not given it.

430
00:17:09,010 --> 00:17:11,550
The study ran for 40 years, until a

431
00:17:11,550 --> 00:17:13,390
reporter exposed it in 1972.

432
00:17:14,810 --> 00:17:18,130
In 1997, the President of the United States

433
00:17:18,130 --> 00:17:19,609
apologized to the survivors.

434
00:17:21,089 --> 00:17:21,710
1941.

435
00:17:22,890 --> 00:17:23,849
War again.

436
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:27,550
The Army builds a giant weapons plant next

437
00:17:27,550 --> 00:17:29,350
to a quiet cotton town in the north

438
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:30,170
called Huntsville.

439
00:17:30,710 --> 00:17:32,710
Its name is Redstone Arsenal.

440
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:36,210
When the war ends, the arsenal goes quiet.

441
00:17:37,370 --> 00:17:39,770
Huntsville is about to lose its biggest employer.

442
00:17:40,910 --> 00:17:44,170
And then, in 1950, the Army sends over

443
00:17:44,170 --> 00:17:46,390
a hundred German engineers to Huntsville.

444
00:17:47,210 --> 00:17:49,390
Huntsville at the time had about 16,000

445
00:17:49,390 --> 00:17:52,510
people, and proudly called itself the watercress capital

446
00:17:52,510 --> 00:17:53,170
of the world.

447
00:17:53,790 --> 00:17:56,410
And these strangers had come to build rockets.

448
00:17:57,050 --> 00:17:59,030
Their leader was Werner von Braun.

449
00:17:59,810 --> 00:18:01,870
In Germany, he had built the V-2,

450
00:18:02,170 --> 00:18:04,670
the world's first long-range ballistic missile.

451
00:18:05,110 --> 00:18:07,450
That is the part of the story Huntsville

452
00:18:07,450 --> 00:18:08,830
took years to face.

453
00:18:09,670 --> 00:18:11,490
Von Braun had been a member of the

454
00:18:11,490 --> 00:18:14,070
Nazi party and an officer in the SS.

455
00:18:14,810 --> 00:18:17,130
His V-2 rockets were built underground by

456
00:18:17,130 --> 00:18:18,990
prisoners from a concentration camp.

457
00:18:19,590 --> 00:18:21,310
Thousands of those prisoners died.

458
00:18:21,870 --> 00:18:23,910
More people died building the V-2 than

459
00:18:23,910 --> 00:18:24,830
were killed by it.

460
00:18:25,530 --> 00:18:27,850
After the war, the United States wanted his

461
00:18:27,850 --> 00:18:29,690
knowledge more than it wanted answers.

462
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:32,450
Questions about his past came much later.

463
00:18:33,330 --> 00:18:35,150
In Huntsville, the team got to work.

464
00:18:35,870 --> 00:18:37,090
January 1958.

465
00:18:37,790 --> 00:18:40,070
A rocket built by the Huntsville team launches

466
00:18:40,070 --> 00:18:43,070
Explorer 1, America's very first satellite.

467
00:18:43,070 --> 00:18:44,070
1960.

468
00:18:44,810 --> 00:18:46,970
NASA opens the Marshall Space Flight Center at

469
00:18:46,970 --> 00:18:47,410
Redstone.

470
00:18:47,970 --> 00:18:50,370
Von Braun is its first director, and they

471
00:18:50,370 --> 00:18:52,670
start drawing the biggest rocket the world had

472
00:18:52,670 --> 00:18:56,110
ever seen, the Saturn V, taller than the

473
00:18:56,110 --> 00:18:58,390
Statue of Liberty, built to take people to

474
00:18:58,390 --> 00:18:58,850
the moon.

475
00:18:59,890 --> 00:19:02,850
The watercress town filled up with engineers, mathematicians,

476
00:19:02,930 --> 00:19:03,570
and machinists.

477
00:19:04,050 --> 00:19:06,670
Within 10 years, Huntsville's population had more than

478
00:19:06,670 --> 00:19:07,310
quadrupled.

479
00:19:07,310 --> 00:19:09,350
They called it the Rocket City.

480
00:19:10,030 --> 00:19:12,970
But a few hours south in Montgomery, on

481
00:19:12,970 --> 00:19:15,610
R Street, something else was about to launch.

482
00:19:16,450 --> 00:19:17,050
1954.

483
00:19:17,930 --> 00:19:20,310
A young preacher arrives to lead a small

484
00:19:20,310 --> 00:19:23,270
brick church on Dexter Avenue, one block down

485
00:19:23,270 --> 00:19:24,190
the hill from the Capitol.

486
00:19:24,870 --> 00:19:26,490
He is 25 years old.

487
00:19:26,990 --> 00:19:30,210
His name is Martin Luther King, Jr. In

488
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:32,430
Montgomery, the city buses had a rule.

489
00:19:32,950 --> 00:19:35,290
Black passengers paid at the front, got off,

490
00:19:35,610 --> 00:19:37,350
walked around, and got back on at the

491
00:19:37,350 --> 00:19:37,830
back door.

492
00:19:38,430 --> 00:19:40,590
And if a white passenger needed a seat,

493
00:19:40,890 --> 00:19:42,550
a black passenger had to stand.

494
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:46,330
In March 1955, a 15-year-old girl

495
00:19:46,330 --> 00:19:48,410
named Claudette Colvin refused to give up her

496
00:19:48,410 --> 00:19:48,630
seat.

497
00:19:49,130 --> 00:19:49,730
She was arrested.

498
00:19:50,730 --> 00:19:53,550
Nine months later, on December 1st, a seamstress

499
00:19:53,550 --> 00:19:55,750
finished work at a department store and walked

500
00:19:55,750 --> 00:19:57,650
to the bus stop, right at the bottom

501
00:19:57,650 --> 00:20:00,650
of R5 blocks, on Court Square, where people

502
00:20:00,650 --> 00:20:01,650
had once been sold.

503
00:20:02,430 --> 00:20:04,190
Her name was Rosa Parks.

504
00:20:04,770 --> 00:20:06,290
When the driver told her to stand up,

505
00:20:06,710 --> 00:20:07,730
she said, no.

506
00:20:08,530 --> 00:20:11,050
Four days later, black Montgomery stopped riding the

507
00:20:11,050 --> 00:20:11,330
buses.

508
00:20:11,970 --> 00:20:14,490
People walked to work, miles, every day, in

509
00:20:14,490 --> 00:20:14,930
the rain.

510
00:20:15,330 --> 00:20:16,630
They organized carpools.

511
00:20:17,230 --> 00:20:19,370
And the boycott was planned in that little

512
00:20:19,370 --> 00:20:21,910
church on Dexter Avenue, with a 26-year

513
00:20:21,910 --> 00:20:23,410
-old Dr. King as its leader.

514
00:20:24,030 --> 00:20:26,830
They kept it up for 381 days.

515
00:20:26,830 --> 00:20:29,110
And in the end, the Supreme Court ruled

516
00:20:29,110 --> 00:20:31,970
that Montgomery's segregated buses were against the Constitution.

517
00:20:32,950 --> 00:20:35,330
The bus boycott was only the first round.

518
00:20:36,230 --> 00:20:39,890
In 1963, Alabama's new governor, George Wallace, stood

519
00:20:39,890 --> 00:20:42,110
on the Capitol steps, on the same spot

520
00:20:42,110 --> 00:20:44,450
where Jefferson Davis had been sworn in, and

521
00:20:44,450 --> 00:20:47,050
promised, quote, segregation forever.

522
00:20:47,810 --> 00:20:49,710
That summer, he stood in a doorway at

523
00:20:49,710 --> 00:20:51,850
the University of Alabama to block two black

524
00:20:51,850 --> 00:20:52,750
students from enrolling.

525
00:20:53,250 --> 00:20:54,890
He stepped aside that same day.

526
00:20:55,550 --> 00:20:58,590
In Birmingham, thousands of children, some still in

527
00:20:58,590 --> 00:21:00,490
grade school, marched for their rights.

528
00:21:01,150 --> 00:21:03,130
The police met them with dogs and fire

529
00:21:03,130 --> 00:21:03,490
hoses.

530
00:21:04,070 --> 00:21:05,810
The pictures went around the world.

531
00:21:06,490 --> 00:21:08,550
September 15, 1963.

532
00:21:09,250 --> 00:21:11,310
A Sunday morning in Birmingham.

533
00:21:12,370 --> 00:21:15,130
A bomb, planted by Ku Klux Klan members,

534
00:21:15,250 --> 00:21:17,910
exploded at the 16th Street Baptist Church.

535
00:21:18,850 --> 00:21:20,290
Four girls were killed.

536
00:21:21,150 --> 00:21:25,330
Addie Mae Collins, Carol Robertson, Cynthia Wesley, Denise

537
00:21:25,330 --> 00:21:25,770
McNair.

538
00:21:26,670 --> 00:21:27,730
Denise was 11.

539
00:21:28,230 --> 00:21:29,550
The others were 14.

540
00:21:30,450 --> 00:21:31,410
Then came Selma.

541
00:21:31,930 --> 00:21:35,090
In Dallas County, around Selma, more than half

542
00:21:35,090 --> 00:21:37,630
the people were black, but only about two

543
00:21:37,630 --> 00:21:39,950
out of every hundred eligible black voters were

544
00:21:39,950 --> 00:21:40,350
registered.

545
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:42,950
Every time people lined up at the courthouse

546
00:21:42,950 --> 00:21:45,730
to register, they were turned away, or arrested.

547
00:21:46,590 --> 00:21:49,970
So they decided to march, 54 miles, from

548
00:21:49,970 --> 00:21:52,450
Selma all the way to Montgomery, to the

549
00:21:52,450 --> 00:21:54,970
Capitol, to the top of our five blocks.

550
00:21:55,850 --> 00:21:58,270
Sunday, March 7, 1965.

551
00:21:59,130 --> 00:22:02,510
About 600 marchers walk out of Selma, two

552
00:22:02,510 --> 00:22:05,130
